SCENARIO: Exact byte boundary on an initial commit; refusal gives actionable split instructions; splitting succeeds
$ git 'init' '-q' '-b' 'main'
[exit 0]
$ git 'config' 'core.hooksPath' '.githooks'
[exit 0]
$ git 'add' '--' 'exact.txt'
[exit 0]
$ git 'commit' '-m' 'Accept exactly 5000 tokens on initial commit'
[main (root-commit) 8164ba0] Accept exactly 5000 tokens on initial commit
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)
 create mode 100644 exact.txt
[exit 0]
[HEAD advanced]
$ git 'add' '--' 'new.txt'
[exit 0]
$ git 'commit' '-m' 'Refuse one byte above 5000 tokens'
precommit-token-cap: 1 staged file(s) exceed the 5000-token limit (tokens = bytes/4)

  new.txt: 20001 bytes = 5001 tokens (limit 5000)

How to split:
  1. Split along existing responsibility boundaries - one concern per file.
  2. Move helpers or sections into sibling files with the same naming convention.
  3. Keep a thin entry point that sources or imports them, then update references and tests.
  4. For documentation, split by heading into linked pages.

An already-oversized existing file only has to stop growing: staging it at the same size or smaller passes, and splitting or shrinking it still helps.
[exit 1]
[HEAD unchanged after refusal]
$ git 'rm' '--cached' '--' 'new.txt'
rm 'new.txt'
[exit 0]
$ git 'add' '--' 'split-one.txt' 'split-two.txt'
[exit 0]
$ git 'commit' '-m' 'Split oversized file into sibling files'
[main 6983a6b] Split oversized file into sibling files
 2 files changed, 2 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 split-one.txt
 create mode 100644 split-two.txt
[exit 0]
[HEAD advanced]
